From 9704a7ae20edfcaffebec2f3617b548e6f310036 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SquadronConsult <michael@defense-dna.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 09:32:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hw/sd/sdhci: no SDMA buffer boundary on the i.MX uSDHC

The uSDHC has no SDMA buffer boundary: the BLK_ATT bits that hold it on a
standard SDHCI are reserved, and an SDMA transfer runs to the end. The
uSDHC write path fills the field with 512 KiB, so a transfer from a
512 KiB aligned address stopped there with a DMA interrupt. U-Boot's
fsl_esdhc_imx never restarts a transfer at a boundary (it does not need
to on the hardware), so any read of more than 1024 blocks into an aligned
buffer, such as loading the kernel, hung until the watchdog reset the
board.
---
 hw/sd/sdhci.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/sd/sdhci.c b/hw/sd/sdhci.c
index b1894af..656d7f8 100644
--- a/hw/sd/sdhci.c
+++ b/hw/sd/sdhci.c
@@ -633,6 +633,16 @@ static void sdhci_sdma_transfer_multi_blocks(SDHCIState *s)
         page_aligned = true;
     }
 
+    /*
+     * The i.MX uSDHC has no SDMA buffer boundary (the BLK_ATT bits that
+     * hold it on a standard SDHCI are reserved): an SDMA transfer runs to
+     * the end without a DMA interrupt in between. U-Boot's fsl_esdhc_imx
+     * driver relies on this and never restarts a transfer at a boundary.
+     */
+    if (object_dynamic_cast(OBJECT(s), TYPE_IMX_USDHC)) {
+        page_aligned = false;
+    }
+
     s->prnsts |= SDHC_DATA_INHIBIT | SDHC_DAT_LINE_ACTIVE;
     if (s->trnmod & SDHC_TRNS_READ) {
         s->prnsts |= SDHC_DOING_READ;
-- 
